# AddressSanitizer suppressions for test/sanitize/run.sh.
#
# Eight entries, one cause, and they are here only because without them the gate
# cannot run at all: ASan aborts the daemon before main() in every single harness
# test, so nothing else would ever be reported.  They are a PRE-EXISTING FINDING
# of the core overhaul's survey, for a later task, not something to suppress and
# forget.
#
# The cause: libgnuworld.a is linked into BOTH the gnuworld executable AND
# libgnuworldcore.so, so every global of libgnuworld exists twice in the running
# program.  ASan's ODR checker is right, and the duplication is not harmless -
# Signal's singleton is six of the eight, so the executable's signal pipe and the
# core library's are different pipes, and ConsoleSink::setEnabled() called through
# one copy does not turn the other off.  The fix is for libgnuworld to be in the
# program once; these entries go away with it, and so does this file.
#
# NOTHING ELSE BELONGS HERE.  A finding that does not stop the gate running is a
# finding to report, not to suppress.  In particular this file does not turn the
# ODR check off (ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_odr_violation=0 would): an ODR violation the
# overhaul introduces still fails the gate.
#
# THE BARE MEMBER NAME IS THE FORM THAT MATCHES, and that is not a style choice.
# ASan matches an "odr_violation:" entry against the name the compiler registered
# the global under, and GCC registers the unqualified source name - 'enabledFlag',
# exactly as ASan's own report prints it.  Checked by running the gate: with the
# entries below the daemon reaches main(), and with the same eight written
# qualified (gnuworld::ConsoleSink::enabledFlag) or mangled
# (_ZN8gnuworld11ConsoleSink11enabledFlagE) it aborts before main() again, because
# neither form matches anything.  Do not "fix" them into either of those.

# libgnuworld/LogSinks.cc: the console sink's enabled flag (line 153) and the
# logger-name column width (line 63)
odr_violation:enabledFlag
odr_violation:nameWidthValue

# libgnuworld/Signal.cc, lines 51-58: the whole of Signal's singleton state
odr_violation:theInstance
odr_violation:signalError
odr_violation:readFD
odr_violation:writeFD
odr_violation:singletonMutex
odr_violation:pipeMutex
